9.954, 59.989, 60.012, 60.105, 60.186, 60.256, 60.325, 60.418, 60.65, 60.848, 61.533]}, "text": "There is a third strand, and it is real in a way the others are not. Visit Mycenae or Tie-rinz, and you can walk beside walls of enormous unworked stones. Beside them, even a hero looks small. Paw-sayneeus, writing in the second century AD, says the walls of Tie-rinz are the work of the Cyclopes, with stones so big that a pair of mules could not move the smallest. The walls date to the Bronze Age, centuries before Homer, and people built them. Archaeologists still call the style cyclopean masonry. The wall is real. The Cyclopes were the Greeks' explanation for it. The Greeks did attach giants to things they could not explain. In Sicily, local tradition says the sea stacks at Ah-chee Tret-sah are the boulders thrown at Odysseus. They are volcanic basalt, about half a million years old. That is legend, not archaeology.", "orig": "There is a third strand, and it is real in a way the others are not. Visit Mycenae or Tiryns, and you can walk beside walls of enormous unworked stones. Beside them, even a hero looks small. Pausanias, writing in the second century AD, says the walls of Tiryns are the work of the Cyclopes, with stones so big that a pair of mules could not move the smallest. The walls date to the Bronze Age, centuries before Homer, and people built them. Archaeologists still call the style cyclopean masonry. The wall is real. The Cyclopes were the Greeks' explanation for it. The Greeks did attach giants to things they could not explain. In Sicily, local tradition says the sea stacks at Aci Trezza are the boulders thrown at Odysseus. They are volcanic basalt, about half a million years old. That is legend, not archaeology."}